A||N||13||Seanh||?'s about removeing engine bay insulation||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||06-05-2001||11:32 AM||||I just cleaned my engine, and got to thinking I was always going to remove that damn insulation in the engine bay, but never have. I am wondering before I start ripping though, is there an con's to removing it? I can't see any advantage to having it there, but I don't know. I just don't want to start ripping it out yet because there really no going back since you have to tear it out. Didn't I hear somthing about this being part of a Fire hazard recall or somthing? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||ROB MAC||06-05-2001||12:37 PM||||That dirty dusty stinky rust promoting crap. I took most of mine out and the only drawback is you lose the sound absorbtion . Engine and road noise are a little louder.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||06-05-2001||12:55 PM||||I'd leave the insulation on the firewall to kill the noise. Trash the rest. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hartz||06-05-2001||12:59 PM||||Yeah, you might do a search, there was a thread a while back on this.  The general consensus was to remove it for asthetics, and a possible aid to cooling.  Sound insulation was the only drawback to removing it.<P>Best of luck,<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||14
Z||000004||Seanh||06-05-2001||01:12 PM||||Guess I must have missed that thread, I haven't been on the forum latly nearly as much as I used to be. I guess I'm gonna pull it then. I will leave the stuff on the firewall cuz I can't stand the road noise as it is, couldn't possible stand anymore. I'm going to gut the interior soon and lay down a good 4 layers of sound matting. This new car is so loud compared to my 86, which already has 4-6 layers of matting on the interior. Its almost as quite as a caddy inside, sounds like a 'vette outside.||||reg||1
Z||000005||84Bill||06-05-2001||03:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Guess I must have missed that thread, I haven't been on the forum latly nearly as much as I used to be. I guess I'm gonna pull it then. I will leave the stuff on the firewall cuz I can't stand the road noise as it is, couldn't possible stand anymore. I'm going to gut the interior soon and lay down a good 4 layers of sound matting. This new car is so loud compared to my 86, which already has 4-6 layers of matting on the interior. Its almost as quite as a caddy inside, sounds like a 'vette outside.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Realllly now. Where did you get the sound matting from? and what is it made of?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||06-05-2001||04:27 PM||||I got the matting from my shop here, its used in stereo installs all the time. Its rubber backed with aluminum and adhesive. Its a real pain to do, but I really think the result is worth the work. Even been next to a car with a big 'ol sound system and the outside of the car is rattling like crazy and really annoying? Thats what this stuff it used to prevent, along with greatly reducing road noise.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||06-05-2001||05:15 PM||||Is your material something like Dynamat? Is it the same thickness? How thick will be the 4 to 6 layers? At least with Dynamat I guess it would be over 1" thick if you put that many layers.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||06-05-2001||05:19 PM||||Yes, it is just like Dynamat. The stuff I used is made by "Tsunami". Its a little smaller then Dynamat, so thats why I used so many layers. Plus I removed the factory insulation, so that gave me the needed room. I would say all and all it comes to about 5/8"-3/4" thick, I haven't really measured it. I used 4 layers on all the interior, 2 layers on the doors, 6 layers on firewall, and 1 layer under the headliner.||||reg||1
Z||000009||johnt671||06-05-2001||07:07 PM||||I took it all out excpt the firewall part and found some rust under it.  No problems , no louder road noise and removing it exposed some additional openings to let more air in.  Sure was dirty!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||06-05-2001||08:18 PM||||Alright, its all out. I did find some rust under there, don't know what to do about it, any ideas? Perhaps just coat it with rust stoping primer or somthing. Man, that was filty under there. My hands are in a little pain from the fiberglass, but nothing I haven't experienced before. I don't hear any extra noise, but as long as it doesn't enter the cabin I don't mind extra outside noise. I think I'm going to remove the cat tonight also, hopfully that may solve my bad gas milage problems. Thanks<P>To anyone thinking about doing this, I say go for it. I am pleased with how much better it looks since that rotten torn up crap is outta there.||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroJoe||06-05-2001||10:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>To anyone thinking about doing this, I say go for it. I am pleased with how much better it looks since that rotten torn up crap is outta there.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>But the only place that it is rotten and torn up is on my firewall!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Talk about a paradox.  I'm not going to be too worried about noise, I can always add some of that dynamat stuff if it gets too bad, but what about heat management?  All the wiring looms in that area are already covered in special reflective tape for that purpose.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||06-05-2001||11:23 PM||||I don't think there is any other con's to removeing the firewall insulation except the increased sound. There is a metal heat shield to keep the exhaust heat away from the firewall and wires on it, and gas tank and lines. I would be more concerned with maybe heat transfer into the cabin perhaps.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierohoho||06-06-2001||10:06 AM||||If you want to pull the insulation I suggest wetting it with the hose first, it helps keep the dust down and limits the amount of particulate fiberglass you get on your hands. If you do it dry wear gloves and a dust mask.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
